      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the remoulade and fry the shrimp"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the remoulade: combine mayonnaise, Dijon mustard, hot sauce, capers, and lemon juice. Season with salt and pepper. Refrigerate until needed."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Soak the prawns in the buttermilk for 10 minutes. Mix the flour, cornmeal, cayenne, salt, and pepper in a bowl."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the sunflower oil in a deep pan to 180°C (a cube of bread should turn golden in 30 seconds). Drain the prawns from the buttermilk, coat in the flour-cornmeal mixture, and fry in batches for 2 to 3 minutes until golden and crispy. Drain on kitchen paper."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Build the po-boy"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spread remoulade on both cut sides of the bread. Layer shredded lettuce and sliced tomato on the base. Pile the fried shrimp on top. Close the sandwich and serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Troubleshooting"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Shrimp coating is soggy rather than crispy",
              "cause": "Oil was not hot enough, or too many shrimp were added at once, dropping the oil temperature",
              "fix": "Fry in small batches. The oil temperature must recover between batches. Drain on kitchen paper immediately after frying."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Po-boys are the everyday sandwich of New Orleans. Every diner, lunch counter, and sandwich shop serves them in multiple versions: shrimp, oyster, catfish, roast beef (debris po-boy), or meatball. Ordering 'dressed' means you want the full lettuce, tomato, and sauce. Anything else and you specify. The soft French bread used in New Orleans is a local style: softer crust than a classic baguette, slightly springier crumb."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
